Identifiability of Routing Guarantees Under Bounded Disclosure in Dynamic Multi-Domain Space Networks

A Partial-Identification Framework for Quantitative Verification Under Private Dynamic State

Ouadi Maakoul + AI Collaborative Research

---

Abstract

We develop a partial-identification framework for inter-domain routing in multi-domain space networks where each administrative domain keeps its internal network state private. Rather than reconstructing exact internal state — the objective of secure multi-party computation approaches — we characterize the set of routing-guarantee values compatible with a bounded disclosure. The framework is distribution-free and mechanism-agnostic: disclosure is parameterized by compatible state sets \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) rather than by any specific privacy mechanism (differential privacy, interval bounds, entropy leakage, cryptographic commitment).

We establish the following results. (i) A three-valued verification structure — SATISFIED, UNRESOLVED, VIOLATED — for quantitative routing guarantees under bounded disclosure, replacing the binary identifiability dichotomy. (ii) A residual-identification proposition for additively separable path metrics: predictable orbital geometry subtracts a known deterministic component from the identification problem but does not eliminate residual private uncertainty. (iii) A counterexample showing that bottleneck metrics do not decompose additively, together with the correct edge-wise bound. (iv) A monotone verification-breakdown threshold \kappa^* characterizing the maximum disclosure level at which a guarantee remains certifiable as SATISFIED. (v) A complexity separation between verification of a fixed path (O(|\mathcal{P}|) for separable metrics) and robust route selection (problem-dependent).

The framework is analytical. No protocols are proposed; no experiments are performed. We position the contribution against SPIDeR, Seagull, IVeri, and AegisPath, and we state precisely what is standard mathematics, what is adopted modeling, and what is a domain-specific contribution.

