1. Introduction

1.1 The Identifiability Question

Independently operated satellite networks must exchange traffic across dynamically evolving orbital topologies while keeping internal network state private. Existing privacy-preserving approaches answer specific questions — control-plane policy compliance (SPIDeR), configuration correctness (Seagull, IVeri), forwarding behavior (AegisPath) — but do not characterize the space of answerable questions: given a bounded disclosure, what can be known about a quantitative routing guarantee?

This paper addresses that characterization problem. We do not propose a protocol, a cryptographic mechanism, or a routing algorithm. We ask: given a private dynamic state S and a bounded disclosure \phi(S), what is the set of routing-guarantee values consistent with the disclosure? The framework that answers this question is the partial-identification framework of Manski, adapted to the routing problem.

1.2 Scope

The scope is restricted to the intersection of:

\boxed{\text{dynamic inter-domain routing} + \text{private internal state} + \text{quantitative service guarantees}}

We do not claim novelty in orbital communication layers, inter-satellite optical links, delay-tolerant networking, generic inter-domain routing, generic privacy-preserving routing, or any specific cryptographic primitive. No protocol novelty is claimed because the application domain is space.

1.3 Contributions by Tier

We organize contributions by their epistemic status.

Tier A — Established mathematics. The identifiability criterion (Proposition 3.1) and the indistinguishability impossibility (Proposition 3.2) are standard applications of statistical partial identification.

Tier B — Domain adaptation. The identified set for routing targets (Section 4), the three-valued verification structure (Section 5), and the selection–verification separation (Proposition 5.2) adapt partial-identification principles to the routing problem.

Tier C — Space-specific structure. The residual decomposition for additively separable metrics (Proposition 6.1) and the bottleneck-metric correction (Section 6.3) apply to predictable orbital dynamics.

Tier D — New theorem target. The verification-breakdown threshold \kappa^* (Section 7) and its monotonicity (Proposition 7.1) are the sharpest formal objects and the target for further mathematical work.

Tier E — Complexity separation. Verification of a fixed path is O(|\mathcal{P}|); robust route selection is problem-dependent (Section 8). No universal MPC complexity claim is made.

