2.2 Relation to Partial Identification

Manski's framework characterizes the set of parameter values consistent with observations and maintained assumptions. The central objects are the identified set and its diameter. Our framework instantiates this structure with:

· Parameter: the internal link-state matrix \mathbf{W}.
· Observation: the disclosed abstraction \phi(\mathbf{W}).
· Identified set: the collection of routing-guarantee values compatible with the disclosure.

The mathematics is standard. The adaptation to a distributed, time-varying, physically structured routing problem is the domain contribution.

2.3 Relation to Predictable Mobile Networks and Time-Expanded Graphs

Predictable-mobile-network research formalizes spacecraft topology as sequences of static snapshots, distinguishing scheduled changes from stochastic failures. Time-Expanded Graphs (TEGs) are established tools for dynamic space-information networks and LEO topology optimization.

We adopt both without claiming novelty. The question we ask is:

\boxed{\text{What does the predictable-snapshot structure imply for identification under private state?}}

This is not the same as proposing a new routing protocol for predictable networks.

