3. Formal Model

3.1 States and Disclosures

Let \mathcal{S} be the state space — the set of physically realizable network configurations. A disclosure mechanism is a family of maps

\mathcal{M}_\kappa: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{Y}_\kappa

parameterized by a disclosure level \kappa \in \mathcal{K}, where \mathcal{K} is a partially ordered index set. The interpretation of \kappa is left open: it may represent interval width, differential-privacy budget, quantization resolution, entropy leakage, or any other scalar mechanism parameter.

For a disclosed message y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa, the compatible set is

\mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \{s \in \mathcal{S} : \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s) = y\}.

Assumption 3.1 (Monotonicity). The family \{\mathcal{C}_\kappa\} is monotone in \kappa:

\kappa_1 \preceq \kappa_2 \implies \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_1}(y) \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_2}(y)

for compatible y. We interpret larger \kappa as coarser disclosure (larger compatible set). Where a privacy interpretation is desired, the direction is inverted by convention: coarser disclosure corresponds to stronger privacy.

Remark 3.1. Parameterizing disclosure by compatible sets rather than by a mechanism-specific parameter (e.g., DP (\epsilon, \delta)) avoids the ambiguity of comparing mechanisms with different semantics. Differential privacy, interval disclosure, and entropy leakage are not interchangeable; the framework requires only Assumption 3.1.

3.2 Routing Targets and Identified Sets

Let T: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{T} be a routing target. The identified set at disclosure y and level \kappa is

\Theta_T(y; \kappa) = \{T(s) : s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)\}.

Proposition 3.1 (Identifiability Criterion). A target T is exactly identifiable at level \kappa iff for all y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa,

|\Theta_T(y; \kappa)| = 1.

Equivalently: for all s_1, s_2 with \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_1) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_2), T(s_1) = T(s_2).

Proof. If T factors through \mathcal{M}_\kappa, then T = \hat{T} \circ \mathcal{M}_\kappa for some \hat{T}: \mathcal{Y}_\kappa \to \mathcal{T}; hence \Theta_T(y; \kappa) = \{\hat{T}(y)\}, a singleton. Conversely, if every identified set is a singleton, define \hat{T}(y) to be its unique element; then T(s) = \hat{T}(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s)) for all s. ∎

Proposition 3.2 (Indistinguishability Impossibility). If there exist s_a, s_b \in \mathcal{S} with \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b) and T(s_a) \neq T(s_b), then no verifier V: \mathcal{Y}_\kappa \to \mathcal{T} can be simultaneously sound and complete for T at level \kappa.

Proof. V receives identical input \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b), so V(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a)) = V(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b)). But T(s_a) \neq T(s_b), so V disagrees with T on at least one state. ∎

Remark 3.2. Propositions 3.1 and 3.2 are standard. They are included to fix notation and make the framework self-contained.

3.3 Time-Expanded Graph Representation

Let \mathcal{T} = \{t_1, \ldots, t_K\} be a discretized time horizon. The orbital multi-domain network is represented as a Time-Expanded Graph

\mathcal{G}(\mathcal{T}) = \left(\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}(\mathcal{T}), \mathbf{W}(\mathcal{T})\right)

with \mathcal{V} the set of satellite nodes and ground stations across domains \mathcal{D}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{D}_M; \mathcal{E}(t) the active inter-satellite and space-to-ground links at time t; and \mathbf{W}(t) \in \mathbb{R}_+^{|\mathcal{E}(t)| \times k} the non-negative k-attribute link-state matrix.

This representation is adopted from the existing literature. It is not a contribution.

3.4 Path Metrics

Let \mathcal{P}_{s \to d} be a path from source s to destination d. A path metric J(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}) is:

· Additive if J(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}) = \sum_{e \in \mathcal{P}} J_e(\mathbf{W}_e). Example: end-to-end latency.
· Bottleneck if J(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}) = \min_{e \in \mathcal{P}} J_e(\mathbf{W}_e). Example: end-to-end available bandwidth.
· Coupled otherwise.

The two separable families require different treatment, as shown in Section 6.

