4. Partial Identification of Routing Guarantees

4.1 Identified Set for a Quantitative Guarantee

Fix a path \mathcal{P} and a threshold \eta. Define the binary target

T_\eta(\mathcal{P}, s) = \mathbf{1}[J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta].

The identified set is

\Theta_{T_\eta}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \{T_\eta(\mathcal{P}, s) : s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)\} \subseteq \{0, 1\}.

By Proposition 3.1, the guarantee is exactly identifiable at level \kappa iff \Theta_{T_\eta} is a singleton for every y. This partitions the disclosure space into three regions:

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\Theta_{T_\eta} = \{1\} &\implies \text{SATISFIED (certifiable)}, \\
\Theta_{T_\eta} = \{0\} &\implies \text{VIOLATED (certifiable)}, \\
\Theta_{T_\eta} = \{0, 1\} &\implies \text{UNRESOLVED}.
\end{aligned}}

4.2 Endpoint Formulation

For a continuous path metric J, define the upper and lower endpoints of the identified interval:

\overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \sup_{s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J(\mathcal{P}, s), \qquad
\underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \inf_{s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J(\mathcal{P}, s).

Then

\Theta_{T_\eta}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) =
\begin{cases}
\{1\} & \text{if } \overline{J} \leq \eta, \\
\{0\} & \text{if } \underline{J} > \eta, \\
\{0, 1\} & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

The identification width is

W_J(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) - \underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa).

The guarantee is SATISFIED iff \overline{J} \leq \eta; equivalently, iff W_J \leq \eta - \underline{J}. Exact identification of the binary target corresponds to W_J placing [\underline{J}, \overline{J}] entirely below or entirely above \eta.

Remark 4.1. The three-valued structure is the standard trichotomy of interval arithmetic applied to a threshold. Its application to routing guarantees under bounded disclosure is the domain-specific contribution.

4.3 Selection Versus Verification

Define two distinct targets:

Selection target:

T_{\mathrm{sel}}(s) = \mathbf{1}[\exists \mathcal{P} \in \mathcal{R}_{s \to d} : J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta].

Verification target for a specific path \mathcal{P}:

T_{\mathrm{ver}}(s, \mathcal{P}) = \mathbf{1}[J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta].

Proposition 4.1 (Selection–Verification Gap). \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{sel}}}(y; \kappa) = \{1\} does not imply \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{ver}}}(y, \mathcal{P}; \kappa) = \{1\} for any particular \mathcal{P} \in \mathcal{R}_{s \to d}.

Proof. Construct s_a, s_b \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) such that s_a admits feasible \mathcal{P}_a, s_b admits feasible \mathcal{P}_b \neq \mathcal{P}_a, and neither state admits the other's path. Then T_{\mathrm{sel}}(s_a) = T_{\mathrm{sel}}(s_b) = 1, so \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{sel}}}(y; \kappa) = \{1\}. But for \mathcal{P} = \mathcal{P}_a, T_{\mathrm{ver}}(s_a, \mathcal{P}_a) = 1 and T_{\mathrm{ver}}(s_b, \mathcal{P}_a) = 0, so \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{ver}}}(y, \mathcal{P}_a; \kappa) = \{0, 1\}. ∎

Interpretation. Existence of a feasible path may be identifiable while verification that a particular executed path was feasible is not. The gap is structural, not probabilistic.

