5. Three-Valued Verification Architecture

5.1 The Verification Decision

The three-valued output of Section 4 suggests a verification architecture in which the verifier returns one of:

\text{Verify}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) \in \{\texttt{SAT}, \texttt{UNR}, \texttt{VIO}\}.

The decision rule is:

\text{Verify}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) =
\begin{cases}
\texttt{SAT} & \text{if } \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) \leq \eta, \\
\texttt{VIO} & \text{if } \underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) > \eta, \\
\texttt{UNR} & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

5.2 Soundness and Completeness

Definition 5.1. A verifier V is sound if V(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \texttt{SAT} implies J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta for every s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y). It is complete if J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta for every s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) implies V(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \texttt{SAT}.

Proposition 5.1. The three-valued verifier is sound and complete for the SATISFIED region.

Proof. Soundness: If \overline{J} \leq \eta, then for every s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y), J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \overline{J} \leq \eta. Completeness: If J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta for every s, then in particular the supremum satisfies \overline{J} \leq \eta. ∎

Remark 5.1. The verifier is not complete for VIOLATED and UNRESOLVED in the same sense: it may return UNR when \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) contains both satisfying and violating states. This is not incompleteness but the correct behavior under partial identification.

5.3 Architectural Components

The verification architecture has three logical components:

Domain-side disclosure. Each domain \mathcal{D}_m computes and broadcasts \mathcal{M}_\kappa(\mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{D}_m}) at the topology-update rate.

Cross-domain message aggregation. A verifier aggregates the disclosed messages \{y_m\} and constructs the compatible set \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \prod_m \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_m}(y_m) under the assumption of domain independence.

Path verification. For a candidate path \mathcal{P}, the verifier computes \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) and \underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) and returns the three-valued decision.

No cryptographic operations are required at verification time. No communication is required beyond the disclosure broadcast.

