6. Residual Decomposition for Additive Metrics

6.1 Decomposition of the Link-State Matrix

Decompose

\mathbf{W}(t) = \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}(t) + \mathbf{R}(t)

where \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}} is the publicly predictable component (orbital geometry, free-space path loss, deterministic propagation delay, line-of-sight windows) and \mathbf{R} is the residual private component (queue occupancy, congestion, switching overhead, policy weights).

Remark 6.1. We use residual decomposition, not subspace decomposition. The two components need not lie in complementary vector subspaces; the decomposition is algebraic, not geometric. This avoids implying projection operators or closure properties that are not established.

6.2 Additively Separable Metrics

Proposition 6.1 (Residual Identification for Additive Metrics). Suppose the path metric decomposes as

J(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}) = J_{\mathrm{known}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}) + J_{\mathrm{priv}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{R}),

and the disclosure mechanism has the form

\mathcal{M}_\kappa(\mathbf{W}) = \left(\mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}, \mathcal{M}_\kappa^{\mathrm{priv}}(\mathbf{R})\right).

Then:

\overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = J_{\mathrm{known}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}) + \overline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y_{\mathrm{priv}}; \kappa),

W_J(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = W_{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y_{\mathrm{priv}}; \kappa).

Proof. For fixed \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}, the map \mathbf{R} \mapsto J_{\mathrm{known}} + J_{\mathrm{priv}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{R}) is a translation on \mathbb{R}. Translation preserves diameters and shifts both endpoints by the same constant. ∎

Interpretation. Predictable orbital geometry does not eliminate uncertainty. It subtracts a known constant from the identification problem. The residual width is determined entirely by the disclosure of \mathbf{R}.

6.3 Bottleneck Metrics Do Not Decompose Additively

For bottleneck metrics J(\mathcal{P}) = \min_{e \in \mathcal{P}} J_e, the additive decomposition fails. Even when J_e = J_{e,\mathrm{known}} + J_{e,\mathrm{priv}} for each edge, in general

\min_{e \in \mathcal{P}} (J_{e,\mathrm{known}} + J_{e,\mathrm{priv}}) \neq \min_{e \in \mathcal{P}} J_{e,\mathrm{known}} + \min_{e \in \mathcal{P}} J_{e,\mathrm{priv}}.

Counterexample. Let \mathcal{P} have two edges with

J_{\mathrm{known}} = (10, 2), \qquad J_{\mathrm{priv}} = (0, 10).

Then

\min_e (J_{\mathrm{known}} + J_{\mathrm{priv}}) = \min(10, 12) = 10,

whereas

\min_e J_{\mathrm{known}} + \min_e J_{\mathrm{priv}} = 2 + 0 = 2.

The two values differ by a factor of five.

Correct treatment. For bottleneck metrics with edge-wise independent compatible sets \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \prod_e \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_e}(y_e), define the lower and upper endpoints directly:

\underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \min_{e \in \mathcal{P}} \underline{J_e}(y_e; \kappa_e),

\overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \min_{e \in \mathcal{P}} \overline{J_e}(y_e; \kappa_e).

These are the correct edge-wise bounds for a bottleneck metric. They are computable in O(|\mathcal{P}|) but must be stated separately from the additive case.

6.4 Coupled Metrics

For metrics whose value depends on the joint configuration of multiple edges — for instance, queueing-driven latency where the worst case depends on the joint distribution of arrivals across domains — the endpoints are given by

\overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \sup_{s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J(\mathcal{P}, s),

which is the optimum of a linear (or convex) program over the polytope \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) when the constraint structure is convex. This is polynomial-time solvable but not linear in path length. We do not claim otherwise.

