7. Verification-Breakdown Threshold

7.1 Definition

For a guarantee J(\mathcal{P}) \leq \eta and disclosure level \kappa, define the verification bound

B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) = \sup_{y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa} \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa).

The verification-breakdown threshold is

\kappa^*(\mathcal{P}, \eta) = \sup\{\kappa \in \mathcal{K} : B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) \leq \eta\}.

This is the coarsest disclosure level at which the guarantee remains certifiable as SATISFIED for every compatible state.

Remark 7.1. We use verification-breakdown threshold, not phase transition. The latter term implies a discontinuous change of state that the model does not generally exhibit. A smooth crossing of \eta by B_J is the generic case; discontinuities arise only for specific metric classes.

7.2 Monotonicity

Proposition 7.1. Under Assumption 3.1 (monotonicity of compatible sets) and monotonicity of J in the state, the map \kappa \mapsto B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) is non-decreasing in \kappa (i.e., non-increasing in privacy).

Proof. If \kappa_1 \preceq \kappa_2, then for every y, \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_1}(y) \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_2}(y). The supremum over a superset is at least as large. Hence B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa_1) \leq B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa_2). ∎

Corollary 7.1. The breakdown threshold \kappa^* is well-defined. The guarantee is certifiable as SATISFIED for \kappa \preceq \kappa^* and not certifiable for \kappa \succ \kappa^*.

7.3 Geometric Structure of the Crossing

The nature of the crossing at \kappa^* depends on the metric class:

Additive metrics. For interval disclosure, B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) is continuous in the interval widths. The crossing is smooth.

Bottleneck metrics. Small changes in interval width can flip the argmin over nearly-equal edges, producing a discontinuity in \kappa \mapsto B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa). This is a property of the bottleneck structure, not of the disclosure mechanism.

Coupled metrics. The boundary of the SATISFIED region in disclosure-parameter space may be non-convex or disconnected. The breakdown threshold is then defined per-path rather than globally.

We therefore restrict the term breakdown to the additive and bottleneck cases with non-overlapping intervals. Other cases are handled by direct computation of B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa).

7.4 Interpretation

The breakdown threshold \kappa^* quantifies how coarse a disclosure can be while still supporting certification of a specified guarantee. It is the natural object connecting disclosure policy to verifiability:

· For \kappa \preceq \kappa^*, the disclosure is sufficient and the guarantee can be certified.
· For \kappa \succ \kappa^*, the guarantee cannot be certified from the disclosure alone; additional information must be requested.

The threshold is monotone in \eta: a weaker guarantee (larger \eta) admits a larger \kappa^*. It is also monotone in the metric sensitivity: a metric whose value is more sensitive to residual state has a smaller \kappa^*.

