8. Complexity: Verification Versus Selection

8.1 Verification of a Fixed Path

Proposition 8.1. For additively separable metrics with interval disclosure, verification of a fixed path \mathcal{P} is O(|\mathcal{P}|).

Proof. \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}) is a sum of |\mathcal{P}| terms; comparison with \eta is O(1). ∎

Proposition 8.2. For bottleneck metrics with edge-wise independent intervals, verification of a fixed path \mathcal{P} is O(|\mathcal{P}|).

Proof. \underline{J}(\mathcal{P}) is the minimum of |\mathcal{P}| terms; comparison with \eta is O(1). ∎

The verification complexity is linear in path length for the separable cases. This is the genuine complexity advantage over MPC-based verification, which must evaluate a cryptographic circuit whose depth depends on the routing function being computed.

8.2 Robust Route Selection

Selecting a path \mathcal{P} such that \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) \leq \eta is a robust optimization problem over the graph. For additively separable metrics with interval uncertainty, it is a robust shortest-path problem; solvable in polynomial time but not in general in O(|\mathcal{E}|). For bottleneck metrics, it is a widest-path problem with uncertainty, also polynomial but not linear. For coupled metrics, it may be NP-hard depending on the constraint structure.

We do not claim that route selection is linear. The linear-time claim applies to verification of a fixed path only.

8.3 No Universal MPC Complexity Claim

MPC-based verification executes a function over encrypted state. The cryptographic computation and communication overhead depends on the specific protocol, circuit representation, network size, and security parameters. We do not assert a universal asymptotic lower bound on this overhead. The comparison between our framework and MPC-based systems is between distinct computational problems — verification of a fixed path versus cryptographic evaluation of a routing function — and cannot be reduced to a single complexity class without a precisely defined computation model.

