9. Architectural Design

9.1 Domain-Side Disclosure Interface

Each domain \mathcal{D}_m maintains a disclosure policy mapping its private state to a disclosed message:

\mathcal{M}_{\kappa_m}: \mathcal{S}_m \to \mathcal{Y}_{\kappa_m}.

The policy is characterized by:

· A disclosure level \kappa_m \in \mathcal{K}, chosen by the domain based on its privacy requirements and the guarantees it wishes to support.
· A disclosure mechanism consistent with Assumption 3.1 (monotonicity), such as interval disclosure, quantized disclosure, or cryptographic commitment.
· A broadcast schedule at the topology-update rate.

The interface requires no per-query interaction. The domain broadcasts \mathcal{M}_{\kappa_m}(\mathbf{W}_{\mathcal{D}_m}(t)) at time t; the verifier aggregates.

9.2 Cross-Domain Aggregation

Under the assumption of domain independence, the compatible set factorizes:

\mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \prod_{m=1}^M \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_m}(y_m).

This factorization is what makes the architecture scalable: the verifier's compatible set is a product of per-domain sets, each computed locally.

If domain independence fails — for instance, if two domains coordinate their private states — the factorization is invalid and the compatible set must be computed jointly. The framework does not assume coordination; the factorization is a conditional architectural simplification.

9.3 Verification Layer

The verification layer computes, for a candidate path \mathcal{P}:

1. Deterministic component: J_{\mathrm{known}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}) from public orbital data. Complexity: O(|\mathcal{P}|).
2. Residual endpoints: \overline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y_{\mathrm{priv}}; \kappa) and \underline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y_{\mathrm{priv}}; \kappa) from disclosed messages. Complexity: O(|\mathcal{P}|) for separable metrics.
3. Three-valued decision: SAT / UNR / VIO based on the endpoints and \eta.

No cryptographic operations. No per-query communication. The verification decision is deterministic and reproducible.

9.4 Design Rationale

The architecture is designed to exploit the monotone structure of the identification problem:

· The disclosure is coarse enough to preserve privacy but structured enough to yield bounded identified sets.
· The verification is deterministic and linear in the separable cases.
· The breakdown threshold \kappa^* gives each domain a principled basis for choosing its disclosure policy: coarser disclosure up to the level at which the guarantees it wishes to support remain certifiable.

The architecture does not require a global trusted controller, complete topology disclosure, or cryptographic mechanism. It requires only compatible sets and monotone disclosure.

