10. Limitations and Falsification

10.1 Limitations

Distribution-free framework. No probability distribution on states is assumed. This yields robustness but precludes probabilistic accuracy statements (e.g., "verification succeeds with probability 1 - \delta").

Additive separability. Proposition 6.1 requires additive separability of the path metric. Bottleneck and coupled metrics require separate treatment (Sections 6.3–6.4).

Honest disclosure. The framework assumes the disclosure mechanism faithfully reports the compatible set. If a domain reports compatible sets that exclude the true state, verification becomes unsound. This is the strategic-disclosure problem.

Static disclosure class. The framework treats \mathcal{M}_\kappa as fixed. Adaptive disclosure — where the domain chooses \kappa per query — is not analyzed.

Domain independence. The product factorization of \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) assumes independent domains. Jointly coordinated private states invalidate the factorization.

10.2 Falsification Conditions

The project should be considered unsuccessful as a novel contribution if:

1. An existing result establishes the verification-breakdown monotonicity under equivalent compatible-set semantics.
2. The residual decomposition for additively separable metrics is already standard in the predictable-mobile-network literature.
3. The three-valued verification structure is already used in network verification under partial state.
4. The selection–verification gap is trivial or already characterized.
5. The verification-complexity separation is subsumed by an existing complexity hierarchy for robust network verification.
6. The problem reduces trivially to static partial identification without loss of relevant structure.

The most plausible falsification vectors are items 2 and 3. The predictable-mobile-network literature has formalized snapshot sequences but does not, to our knowledge, apply them to identification under private state. The three-valued structure is standard in interval arithmetic but its application to routing guarantees under bounded disclosure is the domain-specific contribution.

10.3 The Theorem Collision Audit

The next decisive test is a theorem collision audit across:

\begin{aligned}
&\text{Partial identification} \\
&\to \text{Set identification of graph functionals} \\
&\to \text{Robust network verification} \\
&\to \text{Network tomography under uncertainty} \\
&\to \text{Privacy-preserving quantitative verification} \\
&\to \text{Dynamic / predictable graph uncertainty} \\
&\to \text{Space / NTN-specific results}.
\end{aligned}

For every existing result, record the tuple

(\text{state}, \text{observation}, \text{identified object}, \text{guarantee}, \text{uncertainty}, \text{dynamics}, \text{complexity})

and ask:

\boxed{\text{What theorem remains true here that was not already known?}}

That question determines whether the framework earns its place or is subsumed by existing work.

