Appendix A — Notation and Conventions

A.1 Basic Objects

Symbol Meaning
\mathcal{S} State space (all physically realizable network configurations)
s \in \mathcal{S} A specific internal network state
\mathcal{K} Partially ordered index set of disclosure levels
\kappa \in \mathcal{K} A disclosure level
\mathcal{M}_\kappa: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{Y}_\kappa Disclosure mechanism at level \kappa
y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa A disclosed message
\mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) Compatible set: \{s \in \mathcal{S} : \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s) = y\}
T: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{T} A routing target
\Theta_T(y; \kappa) Identified set: \{T(s) : s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)\}
J(\mathcal{P}, s) Path metric for path \mathcal{P} under state s
\overline{J}, \underline{J} Upper and lower endpoints of J over a compatible set
W_J Identification width: \overline{J} - \underline{J}
B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) Verification bound: \sup_y \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa)
\kappa^*(\mathcal{P}, \eta) Verification-breakdown threshold

A.2 Ordering Convention

We write \kappa_1 \preceq \kappa_2 when \kappa_2 is at least as coarse as \kappa_1 in the disclosure ordering. Under Assumption 3.1 (monotonicity), this implies \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_1}(y) \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_2}(y) for compatible y. If the reader prefers to interpret \kappa as a privacy parameter, the order is reversed by convention: larger privacy corresponds to coarser disclosure. The paper uses the disclosure-order convention throughout, and any privacy interpretation is obtained by reading the order in reverse.

A.3 Measurability Conventions

For finite state spaces, all suprema and infima appearing in the paper are attained and no measure-theoretic regularity is required. For infinite or continuous state spaces, we assume:

· (\mathcal{S}, \Sigma_\mathcal{S}) and (\mathcal{Y}_\kappa, \Sigma_{\mathcal{Y}_\kappa}) are measurable spaces.
· \mathcal{M}_\kappa is (\Sigma_\mathcal{S}, \Sigma_{\mathcal{Y}_\kappa})-measurable.
· J(\mathcal{P}, \cdot) is \Sigma_\mathcal{S}-measurable for every path \mathcal{P}.

These conditions ensure that \overline{J} and \underline{J} are well-defined, and that the identified set \Theta_T(y; \kappa) is a well-defined subset of \mathcal{T}. We do not otherwise invoke measure theory.

A.4 Restriction to Attained Fibers

Throughout, we write \mathcal{Y}_\kappa for the image \mathcal{M}_\kappa(\mathcal{S}). If a symbol y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa appears in a statement, it is understood to be in the image, so \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) \neq \emptyset automatically. This avoids vacuous quantifiers in statements like "|\Theta_T(y; \kappa)| = 1 for all y."

