Appendix B — Standing Assumptions

We collect the assumptions used in the main text and refer to them by label.

Assumption B.1 (Disclosure Monotonicity). For every y in the common refinement of \mathcal{Y}_{\kappa_1} and \mathcal{Y}_{\kappa_2},

\kappa_1 \preceq \kappa_2 \implies \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_1}(y) \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_2}(y).

Assumption B.2 (Metric Monotonicity in State). The path metric J(\mathcal{P}, \cdot) is monotone in the state: for every path \mathcal{P} and states s_1, s_2 \in \mathcal{S}, if s_1 \leq s_2 componentwise (in the natural order on link attributes), then J(\mathcal{P}, s_1) \leq J(\mathcal{P}, s_2). This is satisfied by additive latency and by bottleneck available-bandwidth.

Assumption B.3 (Domain Independence). The full state factorizes as \mathcal{S} = \prod_{m=1}^M \mathcal{S}_m and the disclosure factorizes as \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s) = (\mathcal{M}_\kappa^m(s_m))_{m=1}^M. Under this assumption, the compatible set factorizes as \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \prod_m \mathcal{C}_\kappa^m(y_m).

Assumption B.4 (Additive Metric Separability). The path metric admits the decomposition

J(\mathcal{P}, s) = J_{\mathrm{known}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}) + J_{\mathrm{priv}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{R}(s))

where \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}} is publicly computable and \mathbf{R}(s) is the residual private component.

Assumption B.5 (Edge Independence of Residual Uncertainty). Under interval disclosure at level \kappa, the residual compatible set factorizes as \mathcal{C}_\kappa^{\mathrm{priv}}(y) = \prod_{e \in \mathcal{E}} \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_e}^{\mathrm{priv}}(y_e).

