Appendix C — Auxiliary Lemmas

Lemma C.1 (Fiber Factorization). Under Assumption B.3,

\mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \prod_{m=1}^M \mathcal{C}_\kappa^m(y_m).

Proof. Direct computation:

s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) \iff \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s) = y \iff \left(\mathcal{M}_\kappa^m(s_m)\right)_m = (y_m)_m

\iff \mathcal{M}_\kappa^m(s_m) = y_m \ \forall m \iff s_m \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa^m(y_m) \ \forall m \iff s \in \prod_m \mathcal{C}_\kappa^m(y_m).

This establishes the factorization. ∎

Lemma C.2 (Supremum over Product Sets for Additive Functionals). Let f: \prod_m \mathcal{X}_m \to \mathbb{R} be of the form f(x) = \sum_m f_m(x_m). Then

\sup_{x \in \prod_m \mathcal{X}_m} f(x) = \sum_m \sup_{x_m \in \mathcal{X}_m} f_m(x_m)

and similarly

\inf_{x \in \prod_m \mathcal{X}_m} f(x) = \sum_m \inf_{x_m \in \mathcal{X}_m} f_m(x_m).

Proof. For the supremum: for every x \in \prod_m \mathcal{X}_m, f(x) = \sum_m f_m(x_m) \leq \sum_m \sup_{\mathcal{X}_m} f_m. Hence \sup_{\prod \mathcal{X}_m} f \leq \sum_m \sup f_m. Conversely, for each m and every \epsilon > 0, there exists x_m^\epsilon \in \mathcal{X}_m with f_m(x_m^\epsilon) \geq \sup f_m - \epsilon / M. Then f(x^\epsilon) \geq \sum_m \sup f_m - \epsilon. Taking \epsilon \to 0 gives \sup_{\prod \mathcal{X}_m} f \geq \sum_m \sup f_m. Equality follows. The infimum case is symmetric. ∎

Lemma C.3 (Infimum over Product Sets for Bottleneck Functionals). Let f: \prod_m \mathcal{X}_m \to \mathbb{R} be of the form f(x) = \min_m f_m(x_m). Then

\inf_{x \in \prod_m \mathcal{X}_m} f(x) = \min_m \inf_{x_m \in \mathcal{X}_m} f_m(x_m).

Proof. For every x, f(x) = \min_m f_m(x_m) \geq \min_m \inf_{\mathcal{X}_m} f_m. Hence \inf_{\prod} f \geq \min_m \inf f_m. For the reverse, let m^* = \arg\min_m \inf_{\mathcal{X}_m} f_m and let (x_{m^*}^\epsilon) satisfy f_{m^*}(x_{m^*}^\epsilon) \leq \inf f_{m^*} + \epsilon. For m \neq m^*, choose any x_m \in \mathcal{X}_m. Then f(x^\epsilon) = \min_m f_m(x_m^\epsilon) \leq f_{m^*}(x_{m^*}^\epsilon) \leq \inf f_{m^*} + \epsilon = \min_m \inf f_m + \epsilon. Taking \epsilon \to 0 gives the reverse inequality. ∎

Lemma C.4 (Translation Preserves Diameter). Let A \subseteq \mathbb{R} and c \in \mathbb{R}. Then

\mathrm{diam}(A + c) = \mathrm{diam}(A).

Proof. \mathrm{diam}(A + c) = \sup(A + c) - \inf(A + c) = (\sup A + c) - (\inf A + c) = \sup A - \inf A = \mathrm{diam}(A). ∎

Lemma C.5 (Monotone Supremum under Nested Sets). Let \{\mathcal{X}_\alpha\}_{\alpha \in A} be a family of sets indexed by a partially ordered set A, with \alpha_1 \preceq \alpha_2 \implies \mathcal{X}_{\alpha_1} \subseteq \mathcal{X}_{\alpha_2}. Let f: \bigcup_\alpha \mathcal{X}_\alpha \to \mathbb{R}. Then \alpha \mapsto \sup_{\mathcal{X}_\alpha} f is non-decreasing.

Proof. If \alpha_1 \preceq \alpha_2, then \mathcal{X}_{\alpha_1} \subseteq \mathcal{X}_{\alpha_2}, so \sup_{\mathcal{X}_{\alpha_1}} f \leq \sup_{\mathcal{X}_{\alpha_2}} f. ∎

