Appendix D — Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Identifiability Criterion)

Statement. A target T: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{T} is exactly identifiable from \mathcal{M}_\kappa if and only if |\Theta_T(y; \kappa)| = 1 for every y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa.

Proof. We prove both implications.

(\Leftarrow) Suppose |\Theta_T(y; \kappa)| = 1 for every y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa. Define \hat{T}: \mathcal{Y}_\kappa \to \mathcal{T} by \hat{T}(y) =  the unique element of \Theta_T(y; \kappa). This is well-defined because the identified set is a singleton by hypothesis. For any s \in \mathcal{S}, let y = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s). Then s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y), so T(s) \in \Theta_T(y; \kappa) = \{\hat{T}(y)\}, hence T(s) = \hat{T}(y) = \hat{T}(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s)). Therefore T = \hat{T} \circ \mathcal{M}_\kappa, establishing exact identifiability.

(\Rightarrow) Suppose T is exactly identifiable, so there exists \hat{T}: \mathcal{Y}_\kappa \to \mathcal{T} with T = \hat{T} \circ \mathcal{M}_\kappa. Fix y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa. By the image convention (Appendix A.4), \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) \neq \emptyset. For any s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y), we have \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s) = y, so T(s) = \hat{T}(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s)) = \hat{T}(y). Hence \Theta_T(y; \kappa) = \{T(s) : s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)\} = \{\hat{T}(y)\}, a singleton. ∎

Corollary D.1. T is exactly identifiable iff for all s_1, s_2 \in \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_1) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_2) \implies T(s_1) = T(s_2).

Proof. By Proposition 3.1, exact identifiability is equivalent to |\Theta_T(y; \kappa)| = 1 for all y. If \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_1) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_2) = y, then T(s_1), T(s_2) \in \Theta_T(y; \kappa), which is a singleton, so T(s_1) = T(s_2). Conversely, if the implication holds, then for any y and any s_1, s_2 \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y), T(s_1) = T(s_2); hence \Theta_T(y; \kappa) has at most one element, and since it is non-empty, exactly one. ∎

Remark D.1. The proof is a special case of the standard factorization theorem for functions through quotient maps. The "novel" content of Proposition 3.1, if any, is its application to routing targets under bounded disclosure. The mathematics is standard.

---

Appendix E — Proof of Proposition 3.2 (Indistinguishability Impossibility)

Statement. If there exist s_a, s_b \in \mathcal{S} with \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b) and T(s_a) \neq T(s_b), then no verifier V: \mathcal{Y}_\kappa \to \mathcal{T} is simultaneously sound and complete for T.

Proof. We proceed by contradiction. Suppose V: \mathcal{Y}_\kappa \to \mathcal{T} is a verifier. Let y = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b).

Soundness requires: for every s \in \mathcal{S} and every y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa, if V(y) = t, then T(s) = t for every s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y).

Completeness requires: for every s \in \mathcal{S}, V(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s)) = T(s).

Suppose V is both sound and complete. By completeness applied to s_a: V(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a)) = T(s_a). By completeness applied to s_b: V(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b)) = T(s_b).

Since \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b) = y, we have V(y) = T(s_a) and V(y) = T(s_b). Hence T(s_a) = T(s_b), contradicting the hypothesis T(s_a) \neq T(s_b).

Therefore no such V exists. ∎

Strengthening E.1 (Quantitative Version). Under the hypotheses of Proposition 3.2, for any verifier V: \mathcal{Y}_\kappa \to \mathcal{T}, the error rate

\mathrm{err}(V) = \sup_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \mathbf{1}[V(\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s)) \neq T(s)]

satisfies \mathrm{err}(V) = 1 in the sense that V errs on at least one of s_a, s_b. Moreover, if the two states are a priori indistinguishable from the message, no probabilistic verifier can achieve error probability below 1/2 against a uniform prior on \{s_a, s_b\}.

Proof. The first claim follows because V(y) must differ from either T(s_a) or T(s_b). For the second, under a uniform prior, any deterministic V has expected error \geq 1/2, and by convexity any randomized V has expected error \geq 1/2 as well. ∎

