Appendix F — Measurability of the Endpoints

For infinite state spaces, the endpoints \overline{J} and \underline{J} are suprema and infima over the compatible set \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y), which is a fiber of \mathcal{M}_\kappa. We record conditions under which these are well-defined.

Lemma F.1. Under the measurability conventions of Appendix A.3, for every y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa and every path \mathcal{P}, the quantities \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) and \underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) are well-defined extended reals.

Proof. \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa^{-1}(\{y\}) is measurable because \mathcal{M}_\kappa is measurable and \{y\} is measurable in the discrete \sigma-algebra if \mathcal{Y}_\kappa is countable. If \mathcal{Y}_\kappa is uncountable, we equip it with its Borel \sigma-algebra and require \{y\} to be measurable (which it is if singletons are Borel). Then J(\mathcal{P}, \cdot) restricted to \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) is measurable, and its supremum and infimum over a measurable set are well-defined extended reals. ∎

Lemma F.2 (Attainment in Finite Case). If \mathcal{S} is finite, then for every y \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa and every path \mathcal{P}, the supremum and infimum in the definitions of \overline{J} and \underline{J} are attained.

Proof. \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) \subseteq \mathcal{S} is finite and non-empty. A real-valued function on a finite non-empty set attains its extrema. ∎

Lemma F.3 (Monotone Approximation in Countable Case). If \mathcal{S} is countably infinite and J(\mathcal{P}, \cdot) is bounded on each compatible set, then \overline{J} and \underline{J} are limits of monotone sequences over enumerations of \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y).

Proof. Let (s_n)_{n \geq 1} enumerate \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y). Define \overline{J}_n = \max_{1 \leq i \leq n} J(\mathcal{P}, s_i). This sequence is non-decreasing and bounded above, hence converges to \sup_{\mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J(\mathcal{P}, \cdot) = \overline{J}. The infimum case is symmetric. ∎

These lemmas ensure that the endpoints used throughout the paper are well-defined under mild regularity. No additional measure-theoretic machinery is required for the results proved.

