Appendix G — Proof of Proposition 4.1 (Selection–Verification Gap)

Statement. Let T_{\mathrm{sel}}(s) = \mathbf{1}[\exists \mathcal{P} \in \mathcal{R}_{s \to d} : J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta] and T_{\mathrm{ver}}(s, \mathcal{P}) = \mathbf{1}[J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta] for a specific \mathcal{P}. Then \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{sel}}}(y; \kappa) = \{1\} does not imply \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{ver}}}(y, \mathcal{P}; \kappa) = \{1\} for any particular \mathcal{P}.

Proof. We construct an explicit witness. Let \mathcal{S} be a state space containing at least the following four states:

· s_a^1: route \mathcal{P}_a is feasible, \mathcal{P}_b is not.
· s_a^2: route \mathcal{P}_a is feasible, \mathcal{P}_b is not. (Distinct from s_a^1 in residual state.)
· s_b^1: route \mathcal{P}_b is feasible, \mathcal{P}_a is not.
· s_b^2: route \mathcal{P}_b is feasible, \mathcal{P}_a is not.

Let \mathcal{M}_\kappa be a disclosure that maps all four states to the same message y^*:

\mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a^1) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_a^2) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b^1) = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s_b^2) = y^*.

Then \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y^*) \supseteq \{s_a^1, s_a^2, s_b^1, s_b^2\}.

For selection: T_{\mathrm{sel}}(s_a^1) = T_{\mathrm{sel}}(s_a^2) = 1 (route \mathcal{P}_a is feasible), and T_{\mathrm{sel}}(s_b^1) = T_{\mathrm{sel}}(s_b^2) = 1 (route \mathcal{P}_b is feasible). Hence T_{\mathrm{sel}} is constant on \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y^*), with value 1. Therefore \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{sel}}}(y^*; \kappa) = \{1\}.

For verification with \mathcal{P} = \mathcal{P}_a: T_{\mathrm{ver}}(s_a^1, \mathcal{P}_a) = T_{\mathrm{ver}}(s_a^2, \mathcal{P}_a) = 1, but T_{\mathrm{ver}}(s_b^1, \mathcal{P}_a) = T_{\mathrm{ver}}(s_b^2, \mathcal{P}_a) = 0. Hence T_{\mathrm{ver}}(\cdot, \mathcal{P}_a) takes both values on \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y^*), so \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{ver}}}(y^*, \mathcal{P}_a; \kappa) = \{0, 1\}.

Therefore \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{sel}}}(y^*; \kappa) = \{1\} while \Theta_{T_{\mathrm{ver}}}(y^*, \mathcal{P}_a; \kappa) = \{0, 1\}. This establishes the proposition. ∎

Remark G.1. The construction requires that the disclosure \mathcal{M}_\kappa cannot distinguish between states that admit different feasible paths. This is exactly the regime of bounded disclosure: the disclosure is coarse enough that path identity is not revealed, but fine enough that path existence is. The gap is not an artifact of the proof; it is a structural feature of the disclosure–target pair.

Remark G.2. The proposition can be strengthened: the gap is present whenever there exist two states in the same compatible set whose feasible-path sets are distinct and neither is a subset of the other. The proof above uses the minimal such configuration (two disjoint feasible-path sets).

