Appendix H — Proof of Proposition 5.1 (Soundness and Completeness)

Statement. The three-valued verifier defined by

V(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \begin{cases} \texttt{SAT} & \text{if } \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) \leq \eta \\ \texttt{VIO} & \text{if } \underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) > \eta \\ \texttt{UNR} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}

is sound and complete for the SATISFIED region.

Proof. We verify soundness and completeness separately.

Soundness. Suppose V(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \texttt{SAT}. Then by definition \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) \leq \eta. For any s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y), J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \sup_{s' \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J(\mathcal{P}, s') = \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) \leq \eta. Hence the guarantee J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta holds for every compatible state. Therefore V = \texttt{SAT} implies the guarantee holds universally over \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y).

Completeness. Suppose J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta for every s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y). Then \sup_{s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J(\mathcal{P}, s) \leq \eta, i.e., \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) \leq \eta. By definition V(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \texttt{SAT}. ∎

Remark H.1. The verifier is not claimed to be sound for the VIOLATED region in the same sense. A VIOLATED verdict asserts that the guarantee fails for every compatible state, which is a stronger claim than soundness for SAT. The VIOLATED verdict is complete in the dual sense: if the guarantee fails for every compatible state, then \underline{J} > \eta and the verdict is VIOLATED.

Remark H.2. The three-valued verifier is a decision procedure, not a proof system. Its outputs are deterministic functions of (y, \kappa, \mathcal{P}, \eta). No cryptographic or probabilistic elements are involved.

