Appendix I — Proof of Proposition 6.1 (Residual Identification for Additive Metrics)

Statement. Under Assumptions B.4 (additive metric separability) and with \mathcal{M}_\kappa disclosing \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}} publicly and \mathbf{R} via \mathcal{M}_\kappa^{\mathrm{priv}},

\overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = J_{\mathrm{known}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}) + \overline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa),

W_J(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = W_{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa).

Proof. Let c = J_{\mathrm{known}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}), which is a known constant because \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}} is publicly computable from orbital data. For any s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y),

J(\mathcal{P}, s) = c + J_{\mathrm{priv}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{R}(s)).

Therefore

\overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \sup_{s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} [c + J_{\mathrm{priv}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{R}(s))] = c + \sup_{s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J_{\mathrm{priv}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{R}(s)) = c + \overline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa).

Similarly,

\underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = c + \underline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa).

Subtracting, the identification width satisfies

W_J(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \overline{J} - \underline{J} = (c + \overline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}) - (c + \underline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}) = \overline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}} - \underline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}} = W_{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa).

This establishes both claims. ∎

Corollary I.1 (Residual Verification Bound). Under the same hypotheses, the verification bound satisfies

B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) = J_{\mathrm{known}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}}) + B_{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, \kappa),

where B_{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) = \sup_{y} \overline{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa).

Proof. Taking supremum over y on both sides of the endpoint identity and using the constancy of c. ∎

Corollary I.2 (Breakdown Threshold Invariance). Under the same hypotheses, the breakdown threshold satisfies

\kappa^*(\mathcal{P}, \eta) = \kappa_{\mathrm{priv}}^*(\mathcal{P}, \eta - J_{\mathrm{known}}(\mathcal{P}, \mathbf{W}_{\mathrm{known}})),

where \kappa_{\mathrm{priv}}^* is the breakdown threshold of the residual metric J_{\mathrm{priv}} with effective threshold \eta - J_{\mathrm{known}}.

Proof. By Corollary I.1, B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) \leq \eta iff B_{J_{\mathrm{priv}}}(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) \leq \eta - c. The breakdown threshold is the supremum of \kappa satisfying this inequality. ∎

Interpretation. The predictable orbital component shifts the effective threshold but does not change the structure of the identification problem. The residual width W_{J_{\mathrm{priv}}} is determined entirely by the disclosure of \mathbf{R}. This formalizes the claim that orbital predictability subtracts a known constant but does not eliminate residual uncertainty.

