Appendix K — Proof of Proposition 7.1 and Corollary 7.1

Statement (Proposition 7.1). Under Assumption B.1 (disclosure monotonicity) and Assumption B.2 (metric monotonicity in state), the map \kappa \mapsto B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) is non-decreasing: \kappa_1 \preceq \kappa_2 \implies B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa_1) \leq B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa_2).

Proof. Assume a common message space \mathcal{Y} with \mathcal{M}_\kappa: \mathcal{S} \to \mathcal{Y} for all \kappa; the general case with refinement maps is analogous (see Remark K.1 below).

For each y \in \mathcal{Y} and \kappa, define \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \sup_{s \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J(\mathcal{P}, s). Under Assumption B.1, \kappa_1 \preceq \kappa_2 \implies \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_1}(y) \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{\kappa_2}(y). By Lemma C.5, \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa_1) \leq \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa_2). Taking the supremum over y,

B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa_1) = \sup_y \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa_1) \leq \sup_y \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa_2) = B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa_2).

This establishes monotonicity. ∎

Remark K.1. When the message space depends on \kappa, we assume a refinement map \pi_{\kappa_1, \kappa_2}: \mathcal{Y}_{\kappa_2} \to \mathcal{Y}_{\kappa_1} for \kappa_1 \preceq \kappa_2 such that \pi_{\kappa_1, \kappa_2} \circ \mathcal{M}_{\kappa_2} = \mathcal{M}_{\kappa_1}. This is a natural assumption for nested disclosure mechanisms (e.g., coarsening a partition). Under this structure, the proof above goes through with y_2 \in \mathcal{Y}_{\kappa_2} and y_1 = \pi_{\kappa_1, \kappa_2}(y_2).

Statement (Corollary 7.1). The breakdown threshold \kappa^*(\mathcal{P}, \eta) = \sup\{\kappa \in \mathcal{K} : B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) \leq \eta\} is well-defined, and the guarantee is certifiable as SATISFIED for \kappa \preceq \kappa^* and not certifiable for \kappa \succ \kappa^*.

Proof. By Proposition 7.1, B_J(\mathcal{P}, \cdot) is monotone in \kappa. The set \{\kappa : B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) \leq \eta\} is therefore a down-set in \mathcal{K} (i.e., closed under \preceq). Its supremum \kappa^* is well-defined in the completion of \mathcal{K}. For any \kappa \preceq \kappa^*, B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) \leq \eta by monotonicity and the definition of \kappa^*. For any \kappa \succ \kappa^*, if B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) \leq \eta, then \kappa would be in the set and \kappa^* would not be the supremum. Hence B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa) > \eta, and the guarantee is not certifiable. ∎

Remark K.2 (Strict vs. Non-Strict Monotonicity). Proposition 7.1 establishes non-decreasing monotonicity. Strict monotonicity (B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa_1) < B_J(\mathcal{P}, \kappa_2) for \kappa_1 \prec \kappa_2) requires additional conditions, e.g., that the metric depends non-trivially on the residual state and that the disclosure does not already saturate the compatible set. We do not claim strict monotonicity.

Remark K.3 (Continuity). Monotonicity does not imply continuity. For additive metrics with interval disclosure, B_J is continuous in the interval widths. For bottleneck metrics, B_J may be discontinuous at the boundary where the argmin of the residual metric switches. This is discussed in Section 7.3.

