Appendix L — Proofs of Propositions 8.1 and 8.2

Statement (Proposition 8.1). For additively separable metrics with interval disclosure, verification of a fixed path \mathcal{P} is O(|\mathcal{P}|).

Proof. Under additive separability, \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \sum_{e \in \mathcal{P}} \overline{J_e}(y_e; \kappa_e). Computing the sum requires |\mathcal{P}| additions. Comparison with \eta is a single operation. Hence the verification complexity is O(|\mathcal{P}|). ∎

Statement (Proposition 8.2). For bottleneck metrics with edge-wise independent intervals, verification of a fixed path \mathcal{P} is O(|\mathcal{P}|).

Proof. Under edge independence, \underline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \min_{e \in \mathcal{P}} \underline{J_e}(y_e; \kappa_e). Computing the minimum requires |\mathcal{P}| - 1 comparisons. Comparison with \eta is O(1). Hence the verification complexity is O(|\mathcal{P}|). ∎

Remark L.1 (Space Complexity). In both cases, the verifier requires only the disclosed intervals for edges on the path, not the entire compatible set. Space complexity is O(|\mathcal{P}|) for storage of the intervals, or O(1) if intervals are processed streaming.

Remark L.2 (Path Length Bounds). The number of edges in a simple path is at most |\mathcal{E}|, so O(|\mathcal{P}|) \subseteq O(|\mathcal{E}|). For the multi-domain setting, if each domain's internal path has length \ell_m and the inter-domain path has M hops, then |\mathcal{P}| = \sum_m \ell_m.

Remark L.3 (Worst-Case vs. Expected). The complexity bounds are worst-case deterministic. No probabilistic analysis is required. This contrasts with cryptographic verification systems, where complexity is often measured in terms of expected rounds or amortized per-operation cost.

