Appendix M — Coupled Metrics: Convex Programming Formulation

When the path metric is neither additive nor bottleneck but depends on the joint configuration of multiple edges, the endpoints \overline{J} and \underline{J} are computed by solving an optimization problem over the compatible set.

M.1 General Formulation

Assume the compatible set \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) is a convex subset of \mathbb{R}^n. This holds, for example, under interval disclosure, where \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) is a box. The endpoint computation is

\overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y; \kappa) = \max_{x \in \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y)} J(\mathcal{P}, x).

M.2 Convex Case

If J(\mathcal{P}, \cdot) is convex and \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) is convex, this is a convex program. It is solvable in polynomial time via interior-point methods; the complexity is O(n^3 \log(1/\epsilon)) for n-dimensional problems and accuracy \epsilon.

M.3 Non-Convex Case

If J(\mathcal{P}, \cdot) is non-convex, the problem may be NP-hard. In the routing context, non-convexity arises for metrics involving joint queueing effects (e.g., shared buffers across links) or for discrete state spaces with combinatorial constraints.

M.4 Example: Queueing Latency

Consider a two-link path with queueing delays that interact via a shared scheduler. The end-to-end latency is

J(x_1, x_2) = \frac{x_1}{1 - x_1} + \frac{x_2}{1 - x_2} + \alpha \cdot \frac{x_1 x_2}{1 - x_1 x_2},

where x_1, x_2 are utilization levels in [0, 1) and \alpha > 0 is a coupling parameter. Under interval disclosure x_e \in [x_e^-, x_e^+], the endpoint \overline{J} is a convex program if the third term is convex in (x_1, x_2). This is not automatic; the coupling structure determines convexity.

M.5 Note on Tightness

The convex programming bound is tight if the program's optimum is attained at a vertex of \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y). For interval disclosure, the vertices are the 2^n corners of the box. The optimum may or may not be at a vertex, depending on the curvature of J. We make no general claim about tightness.

