Appendix N — Domain Independence and Product Factorization

N.1 The Factorization Assumption

Under Assumption B.3, the compatible set factorizes:

\mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \prod_{m=1}^M \mathcal{C}_\kappa^m(y_m).

This factorization is what makes the architectural design of Section 9 scalable: each domain computes its own \mathcal{C}_\kappa^m(y_m) locally, and the verifier takes the product.

N.2 Implications for Endpoints

Under product factorization, the endpoint computations simplify as recorded in Lemma C.2 (additive metrics) and Lemma C.3 (bottleneck metrics). Specifically:

· For additive J(\mathcal{P}, s) = \sum_e J_e(s_e), if the path edges partition across domains such that each J_e depends only on one domain's state, then the endpoint is a sum of per-domain suprema.
· For bottleneck J(\mathcal{P}, s) = \min_e J_e(s_e), the endpoint is a minimum of per-domain infima.

N.3 When Domain Independence Fails

If two domains coordinate their internal states — for instance, through a shared control plane or a joint optimization — the compatible set does not factorize. The joint compatible set is

\mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) \subseteq \prod_{m=1}^M \mathcal{C}_\kappa^m(y_m),

with strict inclusion possible when the disclosed messages are insufficient to capture the joint correlation.

For the endpoint computation under non-factorizing compatible sets, the problem reduces to a joint optimization over \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y), which may be substantially harder than the product case. We do not characterize the gap in this work.

N.4 Architectural Implication

The architectural design of Section 9 assumes domain independence. If independence cannot be verified — that is, if a domain cannot rule out coordination among other domains — the verifier must either:

· Conservative bound: Use the product upper bound \prod_m \mathcal{C}_\kappa^m(y_m), which yields a larger compatible set and a weaker (but sound) verification verdict. This is sound but may produce false UNRESOLVED verdicts.
· Joint computation: Compute the joint compatible set directly, at higher complexity. This is exact but may be infeasible in a distributed setting.

The choice depends on the verifier's risk tolerance and the availability of joint computation resources.

