Appendix O — Strategic Disclosure: Formal Statement

The framework assumes the disclosure \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s) faithfully reflects the state. In practice, a domain may have an incentive to report a disclosure y' that satisfies the verification predicate without the underlying state satisfying the guarantee. This is the strategic disclosure problem.

O.1 Formal Statement

Let V: \mathcal{Y}_\kappa \to \{\texttt{SAT}, \texttt{UNR}, \texttt{VIO}\} be the verifier. A domain with state s^* and disclosure policy \kappa chooses a reported message y' \in \mathcal{Y}_\kappa to maximize a utility function u(y'; s^*). The verifier receives y' and computes V(\mathcal{P}, y'; \kappa).

Soundness under strategic disclosure. A verifier V is strategically sound if, for every state s^* and every reported message y', V(\mathcal{P}, y'; \kappa) = \texttt{SAT} implies J(\mathcal{P}, s^*) \leq \eta.

Proposition O.1. The verifier of Section 5 is not strategically sound in general.

Proof. Construct s^* with J(\mathcal{P}, s^*) > \eta. Let y' = \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s') for a state s' with J(\mathcal{P}, s') \leq \eta and y' \neq \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s^*). If the domain reports y' instead of \mathcal{M}_\kappa(s^*), the verifier observes y' and computes V(\mathcal{P}, y'; \kappa). If \overline{J}(\mathcal{P}, y'; \kappa) \leq \eta, the verifier returns SAT despite the actual state s^* violating the guarantee. ∎

O.2 Mechanism-Design Perspective

Strategic soundness requires a binding relationship between the reported message and the actual state. Cryptographic commitments (hash trees, Merkle proofs) can provide such binding. The integration of commitments with the partial-identification framework is a mechanism-design problem outside the scope of this paper but a natural extension.

O.3 Open Problem

Open Problem O.1. Characterize the class of disclosure mechanisms \{\mathcal{M}_\kappa\} for which strategic disclosure is either impossible (perfect binding) or yields bounded distortion in the verification verdict. This connects to the mechanism-design literature on truthful revelation and to the cryptographic literature on commitment schemes.

