Appendix Q — Worked Examples

We provide two fully worked examples illustrating the framework.

Q.1 Example 1: Two-Domain Latency Verification

Setup. Two domains D_1 and D_2. Path \mathcal{P} traverses one edge in each domain. The latency decomposes as

J(\mathcal{P}) = J_1 + J_2, \quad J_1 = c_1 + R_1, \quad J_2 = c_2 + R_2,

where c_1 = 20 ms and c_2 = 15 ms are known propagation delays, and R_1, R_2 \in [0, 10] are residual queueing delays. Threshold \eta = 40 ms.

Disclosure. Both domains disclose intervals for their residual delays:

R_1 \in [0, 5] \text{ (from } D_1\text{)}, \quad R_2 \in [0, 5] \text{ (from } D_2\text{)}.

Compatible set. \mathcal{C}_\kappa(y) = \{(R_1, R_2) : R_1 \in [0, 5], R_2 \in [0, 5]\}.

Endpoints.

\underline{J} = 20 + 0 + 15 + 0 = 35 \text{ ms}, \quad \overline{J} = 20 + 5 + 15 + 5 = 45 \text{ ms}.

Identification width. W_J = 45 - 35 = 10 ms.

Verification verdict. \overline{J} = 45 > \eta = 40, so the guarantee is not certifiable as SATISFIED. The verdict is UNRESOLVED.

Breakdown threshold. B_J(\kappa) = c_1 + c_2 + \Delta R_1 + \Delta R_2, where \Delta R_i is the interval width of domain i's residual. Setting B_J = \eta gives \Delta R_1 + \Delta R_2 = 40 - 35 = 5 ms. If \Delta R_1 = \Delta R_2 = 2.5, the breakdown threshold is reached: the guarantee is certifiable up to a residual interval width of 2.5 ms per domain.

Q.2 Example 2: Bottleneck Capacity (Illustrating the Failure)

Setup. Two-edge path with capacities C_1, C_2. Known components C_{1,\text{known}} = 10, C_{2,\text{known}} = 2. Private components C_{1,\text{priv}} \in [0, 5], C_{2,\text{priv}} \in [0, 5].

Additive decomposition attempt. \min_e C_{e,\text{known}} + \min_e C_{e,\text{priv}} = 2 + 0 = 2. This would suggest the bottleneck is at least 2 in the worst case. But the actual bottleneck could be as low as \min(10, 2 + 5) = 7 (if C_{2,\text{priv}} = 5).

Correct edge-wise bound.

\underline{J} = \min_e \underline{C_e} = \min(10, 2) = 2, \quad \overline{J} = \min_e \overline{C_e} = \min(15, 7) = 7.

The identified interval for the bottleneck is [2, 7]. The additive decomposition would have produced [2, 2], which is both incorrect and overly optimistic.

Interpretation. The bottleneck is determined by the minimum across edges, and this minimum depends on the private component of the edge that wins the min. The additive decomposition fails because the argmin can shift depending on the private state.

