Appendix S — Conventions for Future Extensions

S.1 Probabilistic Extension

If a probability distribution \mu on \mathcal{S} is specified, the framework admits probabilistic analogues:

· Bayesian identified set: \Theta_T^\mu(y; \kappa) =  the essential range of T under \mu(\cdot \mid \mathcal{M}_\kappa = y).
· Posterior width: W_J^\mu(y; \kappa) =  the diameter of the essential range.
· Expected width: \mathbb{E}_{y \sim \mu \circ \mathcal{M}_\kappa^{-1}}[W_J^\mu(y; \kappa)].

These are not pursued in this paper. The distribution-free results are strictly weaker than their probabilistic analogues would be, but they do not require an assumption about \mu that may be unjustified in the adversarial multi-domain setting.

S.2 Differential Privacy Extension

If the disclosure mechanism satisfies (\epsilon, \delta)-differential privacy, the compatible sets are replaced by approximate compatible sets:

\mathcal{C}_{\epsilon,\delta}(y) = \{s : \Pr[\mathcal{M}_\epsilon(s) = y] > 0\}.

The identifiability criterion extends with the caveat that "exact" identifiability becomes "approximate" identifiability under the relaxation. This is a natural direction for future work.

S.3 Cryptographic Commitment Extension

If the disclosure is augmented with a cryptographic commitment (e.g., a Merkle root binding the domain to its state), strategic disclosure (Appendix O) becomes detectable. The identifiability framework then applies to the committed state, and the soundness of the verifier is restored. The integration of commitments with the partial-identification framework is a mechanism-design problem.

S.4 Multi-Attribute Extension

The framework treats the state as a vector in \mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{E}| \times k}. For multi-attribute routing (latency, bandwidth, jitter, loss), the identified set is a subset of \mathbb{R}^k rather than \mathbb{R}. The three-valued verdict then becomes a lattice of verdicts indexed by attribute. This extension is straightforward but not pursued here.

